Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Measures for Establishing and Managing the List of Online Service Providers

Fa Fa [2016] No. 23

The Higher People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government, the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army, and the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Measures for Establishing and Managing the List of Online Service Providers are hereby issued for your earnest implementation in light of local conditions.

September 19, 2016

Measures for Establishing and Managing the List of Online Service Providers

To implement the Provisions of the Supreme People's Court on Certain Issues Concerning Online Judicial Auction by People's Courts (hereinafter referred to as the "Online Auction Provisions"), to scientifically establish and manage a nationwide list of online service providers, and to ensure that online judicial auction work proceeds in an orderly manner in accordance with the law, these Measures are formulated.

Article 1 The Supreme People's Court shall establish an Evaluation Committee for the List of Online Service Providers, which shall be responsible for the selection, evaluation, and removal of online service providers. The Evaluation Committee shall be composed of Internet experts, deputies to the National People's Congress, members of the National Committee of the Chinese People's Political Consultative Conference, special supervisors, and personnel from the trial, enforcement, administrative equipment, and technology departments of the Supreme People's Court.

Article 2 Online service providers that can provide an online judicial auction platform meeting the requirements of the Online Auction Provisions and these Measures and intend to conduct online judicial auction business may submit an application to the Evaluation Committee. Those applying for inclusion in the list shall submit an application for inclusion and relevant supporting documents that meet the requirements.

Article 3 Online service providers applying for inclusion in the list shall have information systems, hardware equipment, funds, and personnel sufficient to ensure the safe, convenient, and orderly conduct of online judicial auctions by courts nationwide, and shall provide high-quality services at low prices.

Article 4 Online service providers applying for inclusion in the list shall concurrently meet the following basic requirements:

(1) The online judicial auction platform provided by the online service provider shall have relatively high national visibility and significant influence;

(2) It shall have achieved industry-recognized leading status among similar platforms;

(3) It shall have been conducting online auction business involving public affairs for more than one year;

(4) It shall have no record of violations of laws or regulations.

Article 5 Online service providers applying for inclusion in the list shall be responsible for the security of the online judicial auction platform they provide and shall possess the qualifications and security management systems required by laws and regulations.

Article 6 The online judicial auction platform shall have system openness, technological advancement, and sustainable development capability, be able to achieve information connectivity and sharing with the enforcement case management system of the people's courts, and be able to promptly upgrade and expand services to meet the development needs of online judicial auctions.

Article 7 To ensure the orderly conduct of online judicial auctions, the online judicial auction platform shall meet the following requirements:

(1) Set up a homepage entry and dedicated channel for judicial auctions to enhance user identification and convenience, and through such channel, truthfully, accurately, and completely display all types of information on online judicial auctions to the public;

(2) Have the function of real-time online verification of the identity information of bidders who register for bidding, ensuring that bidders can independently complete registration and randomly generate bidding codes and passwords;

(3) Use an online payment system that has a legitimate operating license and meets security standards, capable of automatically handling the deposit, freezing, and settlement of margin;

(4) Have the function of conducting electronic bidding via the Internet;

(5) Set system operation functions and management authorities for people's courts at different levels, and have automated statistical functions, ensuring that people's courts can release, manage, supervise, and monitor judicial auction activities at any time;

(6) Encrypt and protect all data generated from auctions, ensure security, and have automatic archiving and retention functions, allowing for downloading and making copies;

(7) Have the capability for real-time big data computing analysis, precise targeting, and promotion, ensuring that auction information is promptly pushed to potential bidders to expand the number of participating bidders;

(8) Have multi-terminal login systems and operation functions, including PC and mobile terminals, to facilitate bidders' participation in bidding through multiple channels;

(9) Provide timely and comprehensive consultation, Q&A, reminders, and other services to all parties involved in online judicial auction transactions;

(10) The backend shall not have functions for monitoring bidder information, manipulating, or interfering with the bidding process;

(11) Other requirements deemed necessary by the people's courts.

Article 8 The Evaluation Committee has the authority to examine the relevant procedures of the online judicial auction platform to ensure that the backend has not set up functions for monitoring bidder information, manipulating, or interfering with the bidding process.

Article 9 The Evaluation Committee shall adopt the method of entrusting a third-party evaluation institution to periodically evaluate newly applying online service providers for inclusion in the list, select the best for inclusion in the list and publish the results. Those that waive service fees may be given priority for inclusion in the list. The committee shall annually evaluate the online judicial auction operations of online service providers already included in the list and publish the results.

Article 10 If an online service provider undergoes a significant business change that affects the normal operation of online judicial auction services, it shall promptly report to the Evaluation Committee.

If an online service provider no longer provides an online platform for judicial auctions, it shall apply in writing to the Evaluation Committee for withdrawal from the list three months in advance and shall not unilaterally discontinue services.

Article 11 If an online service provider is found to be in violation of Article 35 of the Online Auction Provisions, the Evaluation Committee, after evaluation, shall remove it from the list and publish the decision.

Article 12 An online service provider that has been removed or permitted to withdraw from the list shall handle the transition and follow-up work properly, including ensuring the smooth completion of ongoing auctions, transferring all stored auction information data to the Evaluation Committee for preservation, and properly handling the transfer and unfreezing of margins.

Article 13 These Measures shall come into effect on September 20, 2016.
